// HAL options for GARUDA: rtl/soc/filelist_chip.f, top garuda_chip_top.
//   irun -hal -64bit -f rtl/soc/filelist_chip.f -top garuda_chip_top -define SYNTHESIS \
//        -nclibdirname sim/static/INCA_libs -l sim/static/hal.log -f flow/2_static/hal.f
//
// GENERATED by tools/hal_deck.py from its table; edit the table, not this file.
//
// The starting point is Cadence's default rule set. 65 rules are switched off below;
// on the chip they produced 91% of the default run's messages (7,528 of 8,274, 2026-10-15).
// Each is in one of three groups, and every rule has its reason on its line:
//   STYLE     layout, naming, portability: no functional meaning on this code base
//   INFO      states a fact about the design, no defect implied
//   REVIEWED  not style; judged not to matter yet, with the task that revisits it
// Everything else stays on: widths, drivers, latches, resets, clocks, clock domains, FSM
// reachability, synthesis and DFT rules. Their findings are held by hal_waivers.txt (errors,
// by rule and file:line) and by hal_baseline.txt (a count per rule that may not rise).
//
// STYLE: layout, naming and portability. No functional meaning on this code base.
//   STYVAL   1173  numeric literal where a named constant is preferred
//   MAXLEN    657  line longer than 80 characters
//   NBGEND    363  begin/end missing around a one-statement block
//   SEPLIN    312  several statements on one line
//   CDNOTE    275  a compiler directive is used (`ifdef SYNTHESIS, `include)
//   CTLCHR    243  control characters in a line (CRLF files); .gitattributes now stores LF
//   OLDALW    142  always @(a or b) written the old way
//   BITUNS    131  constant such as 'h0 without every bit written out
//   ALOWID    129  active-low name not ending in the suffix HAL expects; ours end in _n_i and _n_o
//   NOBLKN    125  unnamed begin/end block
//   PRTCNT    108  module has more ports than the recommended number
//   CDWARN     67  a compiler directive is used (`timescale, `default_nettype)
//   IDLENG     66  identifier length outside 4 to 16 characters
//   LCVARN     59  upper-case letters in a net name
//   NUMSUF     54  numeric suffix in a name
//   PRMBSE     35  parameter written without a base
//   PRMVAL     33  parameter written without a width
//   DECLIN     23  several declarations on one line
//   UCCONN     21  lower-case constant or parameter name
//   EMPSTM      9  empty statement
//   KEYWOD      5  a VHDL reserved word used as a name; the code base is Verilog only
//   EMPBLK      4  empty begin/end block
//   CNSTLT      3  a literal that could be a named constant
//   MPCMPE      3  long expression without parentheses
//
// INFORMATION: states a fact about the design; no defect is implied.
//   FFASRT    581  a flop has an asynchronous reset: every flop of this design that has a reset does, by design
//   INFNOT    504  a wire with no fanout is ignored: the deliberate _unused sinks
//   USEPRT    205  an input port the module does not use: interfaces are kept uniform (shim, wrappers, vendored)
//   DALIAS    146  two wires with the same driver: renaming across a hierarchy
//   FDTHRU     62  an input is a direct feedthrough to an output: readback and decode muxes
//   DIFCLK     33  a clock keeps its function under another name across a port
//   ONPNSG     27  a one-bit bus: the shim's single-event cases
//   IPRTEX     26  a constant in a port connection
//   EXPIPC     23  an expression in a port connection
//   FSMIDN     16  a state machine was recognised
//   TPOUNR      8  an output of the chip top is not a register: the pad drivers; budgets are in the constraints
//   CLKINF      5  a signal was inferred to be a clock
//   USEPAR      4  a parameter that is not used
//   PRMEXP      2  a parameter in a port connection
//   NUMDFF      1  a count of single-bit flops
//
// REVIEWED: not style; judged not to matter yet, with the task that revisits it.
//   CBPAHI    691  combinational path crossing several modules (the pad logic and the shim); matters only for synthesis by block. Revisit: task 3.6
//   SYNPRT    516  an output port assigned from combinational logic; matters for hierarchical timing budgets. Revisit: task 3.6
//   LRGOPR    210  arithmetic or comparison on wide operands; a timing question. Revisit: task 3.6, trial synthesis
//   IGNDLY     60  a lumped delay is ignored by synthesis; all 60 are in vendored i2c_master_byte_ctrl.sv, not modified (D-22)
//   TRNMBT     58  a state changes more than one bit on a transition (binary encoding); a power note. Revisit: task 3.6 power
//   REVROP     56  a register read or written outside its process; pclk_q is the accepted case (SIM-1), the rest vendored
//   SYNASN     48  a module with both synchronous and asynchronous logic: expected with asynchronous-reset flops
//   POIASG     34  possible overflow in an addition; decided formally by Superlint ASG_AR_OVFL (flow/2_static/superlint/RESULTS.md)
//   TSETGV     34  a task assigns a global variable; in id_ex.v and debug_module.v, simulation of register pairs
//   TRUNCZ     33  constant truncation without loss of bits; the width rules of Verilator stay on
//   EXTFSM     15  extra logic mixed into a state-machine process, in vendored controllers; affects state extraction only
//   MXFNOT     12  fanout above HAL's recommendation (MAC cluster, I2C); buffering is a physical-design step. Revisit: task 3.6
//   BADFSM     11  state machine not in HAL's modelling style, in vendored controllers; affects state extraction only
//   VLGMEM     11  Verilog memories in a vendored FIFO; processing time only
//   INDXOP     10  an operator inside an array index (DMA channel select); index range is POOBID, kept
//   SHFTNC      9  shift by a non-constant: the barrel shifters and the ALU, intended; Superlint SHF_IS_OVFL decides it
//   PADMSB      7  a constant padded with zeros on the left; the width rules of Verilator stay on
//   LOOPTM      6  the same operation repeated in a loop; elaboration time only
//   MICAWS      6  several if or case statements in one always block
//   SLNOTP      6  tri-state enable not controllable from a primary input (the pad drivers); a DFT question. Revisit: task 3.7, 3.9
//   REDOPR      4  a reduction operator on a short vector
//   IFSMCD      3  a condition shared by every branch of an if, in the DMA engine and the CSR file
//   TUSEGV      2  a task uses a global variable; debug_module.v
//   MEMSIZ      1  a memory with a one-bit word in the prefetch buffer; it is a flag array, not an error
//   RDOPND      1  a redundant operand in a reduction
//   TROPCZ      1  constant expression truncation without loss; the width rules of Verilator stay on
-halargs "
 -nocheck ALOWID -nocheck BITUNS -nocheck CDNOTE -nocheck CDWARN -nocheck CNSTLT -nocheck CTLCHR -nocheck DECLIN -nocheck EMPBLK
